12:15:14 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Worth pushing back gently on the premise first: "vodka is central to everyday Russian life" is a stereotype, not really an accurate picture of how most Russian families actually live. Plenty of Russians drink moderately or not at all, and daily life doesn't revolve around it any more than any other culture's does. Your partner's family could have totally different habits, tastes, and relationships with alcohol — some households serve it only at celebrations, some rarely touch it, some prefer wine or nothing at all. The much better move: just ask your partner. They know their own family — whether their parents enjoy a drink, what they'd actually like, whether there are any health reasons to avoid it, etc. This also saves you money and awkwardness (nothing weirder than overcompensating for a stereotype and having your guests notice). If you want general hosting tips that apply regardless of nationality: - Have a reasonable range of non-alcoholic options (tea is often huge in Russian households, actually more central than vodka for everyday life — a good tea setup might serve you better). - If you do want to include vodka as a nice touch, one good bottle is plenty for a gesture — you don't need cases of it. - Ask about food preferences too, since that's likely to matter far more for a comfortable visit than the drinks table. Basically: treat them as individuals with their own preferences rather than planning around a generalization. Your partner is the best resource here.
