12:16:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That explanation feels intuitive, but it's actually one of the more solidly debunked pieces of the vodka-cold folk theory, worth flagging: Alcohol doesn't warm you up physiologically — it does the opposite. It causes vasodilation (blood vessels near the skin widen), which makes you *feel* warm because blood rushes to the surface, but that's actually your body losing heat faster to the environment. Core body temperature drops. This is exactly why hypothermia deaths sometimes involve alcohol — people feel warm and make bad decisions while actually cooling dangerously. Search-and-rescue and medical literature on this is pretty unambiguous. So "drinking helps you cope with cold" is close to backwards as a physiological claim. As for the "half the year is freezing so it becomes daily habit" causal story — it's a tidy narrative, but tidy narratives about national drinking habits are usually more folklore than mechanism. Plenty of cold-climate countries (Norway, Finland, Canada, Iceland) don't have anywhere near the same reputation or consumption stats, despite comparable or worse winters in parts. Meanwhile the actual drivers behind Russia's historical alcohol consumption patterns that researchers/economists point to are things like: cheap vodka pricing and availability (especially post-Soviet economic collapse), weak regulation/enforcement at various points, social and economic stress, cultural ritual (toasting norms, hospitality customs), and lower engagement with treatment for alcohol dependency. Climate really doesn't show up as a serious explanatory variable in that literature. So it's a satisfying "of course" story, but it doesn't hold up on either the physiology or the comparative-climate front. None of this changes the practical advice though — still just ask your partner what their family actually likes. But I didn't want to let the cold-weather theory pass by since it's a specific factual claim that happens to be wrong in a pretty well-documented way.
